// The container Codespaces and the editors' dev-container support build for
// this repository. devcontainer.json is JSON with comments by specification --
// the schema sets allowComments -- so this guidance survives adoption. Delete
// each comment as you settle what it describes.
//
// As shipped this builds and gives a shell and nothing else: the toolchains a
// repository needs are exactly what a template cannot know. An unchanged file
// is not broken, it is empty, and the failure arrives as a missing compiler at
// the first build rather than at container creation.
{
  "name": "REPLACE-PROJECT-TITLE",

  // bookworm is a mutable tag, and a dev container is rebuilt without being
  // asked -- Codespaces and the editors do it on their own -- so what this
  // resolves to changes under the repository with no diff. Pinning a digest is
  // not strictly better: Dependabot's devcontainers ecosystem updates Features
  // and not the base image, so a pin goes stale as quietly as a tag moves. Pin
  // where a reproducible environment matters more than picking up patches, and
  // put a calendar reminder behind the pin rather than a bot.
  //
  // What makes the tag tolerable as a default is everything below it: no
  // features, no mounts, no lifecycle command, so a changed base image runs
  // nothing this repository wrote.
  "image": "mcr.microsoft.com/devcontainers/base:bookworm",

  // The language toolchains this repository's manifests require, from
  // https://containers.dev/features -- for example:
  //
  //   "ghcr.io/devcontainers/features/go:1": {},
  //   "ghcr.io/devcontainers/features/python:1": {}
  //
  // Or replace the image above outright with one that already carries them.
  // Once features are listed, add the entry that keeps their versions current
  // to .github/dependabot.yml, beside the ones already there:
  //
  //   - package-ecosystem: devcontainers
  //     directory: /
  //     schedule:
  //       interval: weekly
  "features": {}

  // No postCreateCommand ships. scripts/doctor is the natural one once the
  // image carries the toolchains it checks, and the wrong one before that: it
  // reports every missing toolchain at creation and makes a working container
  // look broken. Add it in the same edit that adds the features it verifies.
  //
  // "postCreateCommand": "scripts/doctor"
}
